  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_CHEMISTRY_BIOMOLECULES_DIAGRAM_Q02 - Structure and amphoteric nature of zwitterion</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The proton-transfer equilibrium of an \(\alpha\)-amino acid in aqueous medium is shown below:</p>
<div style="text-align:center; margin:10px 0;">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 380 180" width="380" height="180" style="background:#ffffff; font-family:sans-serif;">
  <text x="20" y="95" font-size="18" fill="#000000">R—CH—COOH</text>
  <line x1="60" y1="105" x2="60" y2="135" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2"/>
  <text x="50" y="155" font-size="16" fill="#0000ff">:NH<tspan font-size="12" dy="4">2</tspan></text>
  <!-- Equilibrium arrows -->
  <line x1="165" y1="88" x2="215" y2="88" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2"/>
  <polygon points="215,88 205,83 205,93" fill="#000000"/>
  <line x1="215" y1="102" x2="165" y2="102" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2"/>
  <polygon points="165,102 175,97 175,107" fill="#000000"/>
  <!-- Zwitterion -->
  <text x="240" y="95" font-size="18" fill="#000000">R—CH—COO<tspan font-size="14" dy="-8" fill="#d9534f" font-weight="bold">−</tspan></text>
  <line x1="280" y1="105" x2="280" y2="135" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2"/>
  <text x="260" y="155" font-size="16" fill="#0000ff">+<tspan font-size="16" dy="0">NH</tspan><tspan font-size="12" dy="4">3</tspan></text>
  <!-- Box around zwitterion -->
  <rect x="230" y="65" width="140" height="100" fill="none" stroke="#28a745" stroke-dasharray="4" stroke-width="2"/>
  <text x="265" y="55" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#28a745">Species Z</text>
</svg>
</div>
<p>What is the dipolar ionic species designated as "Species Z", and which characteristic behaviour does it demonstrate?</p>]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Step 1</strong><br/>In aqueous solution, the acidic carboxyl group (\(-\mathrm{COOH}\)) of an amino acid loses a proton while the basic amino group (\(-\mathrm{NH_2}\)) accepts a proton within the same molecule.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2</strong><br/>This internal proton transfer creates a dipolar ion carrying both positive (\(-\mathrm{NH_3^+}\)) and negative (\(-\mathrm{COO^-}\)) electrical charges while maintaining net electrical neutrality. This dipolar species is known as a zwitter ion.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3</strong><br/>Because the carboxylate anion can act as a base (accepting a proton) and the ammonium cation can act as an acid (donating a proton), the zwitter ion reacts with both acids and bases, demonstrating amphoteric behaviour.</p>
<p><strong>Final Answer</strong><br/>Species Z is a zwitter ion, and it exhibits amphoteric behaviour.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.2500000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Zwitter ion, behaving exclusively as a strong monoprotic acid</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Zwitter ion, exhibiting amphoteric behaviour</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Anionic salt, displaying exclusively basic properties in solution</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Neutral non-polar molecule, being completely insoluble in aqueous media</p>]]></text></answer>
