  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_CHEMISTRY_BIOMOLECULES_DIAGRAM_Q04 - Glycosidic linkage and nature of sucrose</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The Haworth structural representation of sucrose is given below:</p>
<div style="text-align:center; margin:10px 0;">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 460 210" width="460" height="210" style="background:#ffffff; font-family:sans-serif;">
  <!-- Glucose ring (pyranose) on left -->
  <polygon points="70,70 120,50 170,70 170,120 120,140 70,120" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2"/>
  <circle cx="145" cy="60" r="11" fill="#ffffff"/>
  <text x="141" y="65" font-size="14" font-weight="bold">O</text>
  <text x="175" y="115" font-size="12" fill="#d9534f" font-weight="bold">C1</text>
  <text x="65" y="165" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" fill="#333333">α-D-Glucose</text>
  <!-- Fructose ring (furanose) on right -->
  <polygon points="260,110 240,65 310,40 360,75 330,120" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2"/>
  <circle cx="280" cy="55" r="11" fill="#ffffff"/>
  <text x="276" y="60" font-size="14" font-weight="bold">O</text>
  <text x="245" y="125" font-size="12" fill="#d9534f" font-weight="bold">C2</text>
  <text x="265" y="165" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" fill="#333333">β-D-Fructose</text>
  <!-- Glycosidic oxygen bridge -->
  <line x1="170" y1="120" x2="205" y2="135" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2"/>
  <line x1="205" y1="135" x2="245" y2="120" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2"/>
  <circle cx="205" cy="135" r="10" fill="#ffffff"/>
  <text x="200" y="140" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#000000">O</text>
  <!-- Box around glycosidic link -->
  <rect x="160" y="105" width="95" height="48" fill="none" stroke="#0066cc" stroke-dasharray="3" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="162" y="185" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#0066cc">Linkage Y</text>
</svg>
</div>
<p>Which carbon atoms are joined by the oxide bridge designated as "Linkage Y", and how does this affect the reducing property of sucrose?</p>]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Step 1</strong><br/>In sucrose, the disaccharide is composed of an \(\alpha\)-D-glucopyranose unit and a \(\beta\)-D-fructofuranose unit linked by an oxide bridge.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2</strong><br/>The glycosidic linkage connects C1 (the aldehyde-derived anomeric carbon) of \(\alpha\)-D-glucose with C2 (the ketone-derived anomeric carbon) of \(\beta\)-D-fructose.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3</strong><br/>Because both carbonyl reducing centres are tied up in the formation of the glycosidic bond, neither ring can mutarotate or open up into a free aldehyde or ketone group. Consequently, sucrose is a non-reducing sugar.</p>
<p><strong>Final Answer</strong><br/>Linkage Y connects C1 of \(\alpha\)-D-glucose and C2 of \(\beta\)-D-fructose, making sucrose a non-reducing sugar because both reducing groups are bonded.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.2500000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>C1 of \(\alpha\)-D-glucose and C4 of \(\beta\)-D-fructose; reducing sugar due to a free keto group</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>C1 of \(\beta\)-D-galactose and C4 of \(\beta\)-D-glucose; reducing sugar due to a free aldehyde group</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>C1 of \(\alpha\)-D-glucose and C6 of \(\beta\)-D-fructose; non-reducing sugar due to steric hindrance</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>C1 of \(\alpha\)-D-glucose and C2 of \(\beta\)-D-fructose; non-reducing sugar because both reducing centres are bonded</p>]]></text></answer>
