  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_CHEMISTRY_BIOMOLECULES_DIAGRAM_Q05 - Phosphodiester bond in dinucleotide chain formation</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The schematic diagram below illustrates a dinucleotide segment formed from two nucleotide units:</p>
<div style="text-align:center; margin:10px 0;">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 380 300" width="380" height="300" style="background:#ffffff; font-family:sans-serif;">
  <!-- Nucleotide 1 (Top) -->
  <polygon points="120,70 170,50 200,80 175,120 135,115" fill="#fffbe6" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2"/>
  <text x="145" y="90" font-size="11" font-weight="bold">SUGAR</text>
  <text x="210" y="85" font-size="12" fill="#c71585" font-weight="bold">Base (at 1′)</text>
  <line x1="200" y1="80" x2="225" y2="80" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="110" y="65" font-size="10" fill="#333333">5′ CH<tspan font-size="8" dy="2">2</tspan></text>
  <text x="165" y="130" font-size="10" fill="#d9534f" font-weight="bold">3′</text>
  
  <!-- Bridging Phosphate -->
  <rect x="140" y="140" width="70" height="40" rx="6" fill="#ffe4b5" stroke="#d2691e" stroke-width="2"/>
  <text x="148" y="165" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#8b4513">—O—P—O—</text>
  <text x="220" y="165" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#d2691e">Bridge B</text>
  <line x1="175" y1="120" x2="175" y2="140" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2"/>
  
  <!-- Nucleotide 2 (Bottom) -->
  <line x1="175" y1="180" x2="155" y2="210" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2"/>
  <polygon points="120,225 170,205 200,235 175,275 135,270" fill="#fffbe6" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2"/>
  <text x="145" y="245" font-size="11" font-weight="bold">SUGAR</text>
  <text x="210" y="240" font-size="12" fill="#c71585" font-weight="bold">Base (at 1′)</text>
  <line x1="200" y1="235" x2="225" y2="235" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="135" y="205" font-size="10" fill="#333333">5′ CH<tspan font-size="8" dy="2">2</tspan></text>
  <text x="165" y="285" font-size="10" fill="#333333">3′ OH</text>
</svg>
</div>
<p>What is the chemical identity of the covalent linkage designated as "Bridge B" connecting the two adjacent sugar moieties?</p>]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Step 1</strong><br/>Each mononucleotide unit consists of a pentose sugar ring with a heterocyclic purine or pyrimidine base attached at the \(1'\)-position and a phosphate group esterified to the \(5'\)-position.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2</strong><br/>During polynucleotide chain growth, a phosphate residue creates a bridge by forming an ester linkage with the \(3'\)-hydroxyl group of the preceding pentose sugar and a second ester linkage with the \(5'\)-hydroxyl group of the adjacent pentose sugar.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3</strong><br/>Because phosphoric acid is esterified twice to two separate alcoholic hydroxyls, this repeating covalent backbone connection is termed a phosphodiester linkage.</p>
<p><strong>Final Answer</strong><br/>Bridge B is a phosphodiester linkage between the \(3'\) and \(5'\) carbon atoms of the pentose sugars.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.2500000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Phosphodiester linkage between \(3'\) and \(5'\) carbon atoms of the pentose sugars</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Glycosidic bond between \(1'\) carbon of the sugar and nitrogen of the heterocyclic base</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Phosphoanhydride bond between two adjacent phosphoric acid units</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Amide peptide linkage formed by dehydration between amine and carboxylic acid</p>]]></text></answer>
