  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_CHEMISTRY_BIOMOLECULES_DIAGRAM_Q06 - Structural comparison of ribose and deoxyribose</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The Haworth projections of two pentose sugars found in nucleic acids are shown below:</p>
<div style="text-align:center; margin:10px 0;">
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 460 210" width="460" height="210" style="background:#ffffff; font-family:sans-serif;">
  <!-- Sugar I (beta-D-ribose) -->
  <polygon points="60,75 110,45 160,75 145,130 75,130" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2"/>
  <circle cx="110" cy="55" r="10" fill="#ffffff"/>
  <text x="106" y="60" font-size="14" font-weight="bold">O</text>
  <!-- 1' OH up -->
  <line x1="160" y1="75" x2="160" y2="45" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="155" y="40" font-size="12" fill="#800080" font-weight="bold">OH</text>
  <text x="165" y="80" font-size="10">1′</text>
  <!-- 2' OH down -->
  <line x1="145" y1="130" x2="145" y2="155" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="137" y="170" font-size="12" fill="#800080" font-weight="bold">OH</text>
  <text x="150" y="130" font-size="10">2′</text>
  <!-- 3' OH down -->
  <line x1="75" y1="130" x2="75" y2="155" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="67" y="170" font-size="12" fill="#800080" font-weight="bold">OH</text>
  <text x="60" y="130" font-size="10">3′</text>
  <!-- 5' CH2OH -->
  <line x1="60" y1="75" x2="45" y2="45" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="15" y="40" font-size="11">HO—CH<tspan font-size="8" dy="2">2</tspan></text>
  <text x="70" y="195" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" fill="#000000">Structure (I)</text>
  
  <!-- Sugar II (beta-D-2-deoxyribose) -->
  <polygon points="280,75 330,45 380,75 365,130 295,130" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="2"/>
  <circle cx="330" cy="55" r="10" fill="#ffffff"/>
  <text x="326" y="60" font-size="14" font-weight="bold">O</text>
  <!-- 1' OH up -->
  <line x1="380" y1="75" x2="380" y2="45" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="375" y="40" font-size="12" fill="#800080" font-weight="bold">OH</text>
  <text x="385" y="80" font-size="10">1′</text>
  <!-- 2' H only (highlighted) -->
  <line x1="365" y1="130" x2="365" y2="155" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="360" y="170" font-size="13" fill="#d9534f" font-weight="bold">H</text>
  <text x="370" y="130" font-size="10">2′</text>
  <circle cx="365" cy="165" r="12" fill="none" stroke="#d9534f" stroke-dasharray="2" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <!-- 3' OH down -->
  <line x1="295" y1="130" x2="295" y2="155" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="287" y="170" font-size="12" fill="#800080" font-weight="bold">OH</text>
  <text x="280" y="130" font-size="10">3′</text>
  <!-- 5' CH2OH -->
  <line x1="280" y1="75" x2="265" y2="45" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="235" y="40" font-size="11">HO—CH<tspan font-size="8" dy="2">2</tspan></text>
  <text x="290" y="195" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" fill="#000000">Structure (II)</text>
</svg>
</div>
<p>Which statement correctly identifies Structure (I) and Structure (II), along with the specific nucleic acid macromolecule containing Structure (II)?</p>]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Step 1</strong><br/>Structure (I) possesses a five-membered furanose ring containing hydroxyl groups at both the \(2'\) and \(3'\) positions, identifying it as \(\beta\)-D-ribose.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2</strong><br/>Structure (II) differs by having only hydrogen atoms at the \(2'\) carbon (lacking an oxygen atom at position \(2'\)) while maintaining a hydroxyl group at position \(3'\), identifying it as \(\beta\)-D-2-deoxyribose.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3</strong><br/>\(\beta\)-D-ribose serves as the pentose sugar backbone in ribonucleic acid (RNA), whereas \(\beta\)-D-2-deoxyribose is found specifically in deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA).</p>
<p><strong>Final Answer</strong><br/>Structure (I) is \(\beta\)-D-ribose, while Structure (II) is \(\beta\)-D-2-deoxyribose, which is present in DNA.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
